Benign Joint Hypermobility Syndrome
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Generalized joint hypermobility, Chronic widespread joint pain, Atraumatic subluxations, Beighton score >4

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-003
Reason for Visit: Acute-on-chronic widespread joint pain and inability to mobilize safely following a minor atraumatic subluxation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and flatmate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute severe pain following a suspected joint subluxation, rendering the patient practically immobile and unable to manage basic activities of daily living. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to assess joint integrity, rule out an irreducible dislocation or acute neurovascular compromise, and provide immediate pharmacological pain relief to allow safe mobilization within the home prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My knee gave way and popped out again, and now my whole body aches so badly I can't even walk to the bathroom."
History of Present Illness: Patient describes a sudden, atraumatic subluxation of the right patella that spontaneously reduced, followed by a severe flare of chronic, widespread musculoskeletal pain. Patient reports generalized exhaustion and poor physical stamina over the last 24 hours. They note a lifelong history of "double joints," frequent clumsiness due to poor spatial awareness, and easy bruising. Pain is currently preventing weight-bearing.
Functional Status: Temporarily bedbound due to acute pain flare and fear of secondary subluxations.
Social/Safety Concerns: Flatmate works night shifts; patient is highly vulnerable to falls when alone due to acute proprioceptive deficits and pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/72 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Fatigued appearance, lying supine, guarding the right lower limb.
Physical Exam: Beighton score 7/9 on formal clinical assessment (bilateral knees, elbows, thumbs, and forward flexion). Generalized excessive range of motion across multiple joints. Right knee exhibits mild effusion but is completely reduced; ligaments lax but grossly intact. No focal neurovascular deficit in the lower limbs. Skin texture is moderately doughy with scattered resolving ecchymoses on shins, but lacks extreme fragility.
Environmental Observations: Loose rugs on polished floorboards presenting an immediate trip hazard for a patient with proprioceptive deficits.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking PRN paracetamol with minimal effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Benign Joint Hypermobility Syndrome with acute pain flare - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute irreducible dislocation - Ruled out as the joint is visibly and palpably reduced with normal distal pulses and sensation.

Classic Ehlers-Danlos Syndrome - Unlikely due to absence of severe atrophic scarring, extreme skin fragility, and lack of history of cardiovascular complications.

Inflammatory Polyarthritis - Ruled out clinically due to lack of hot, red, swollen joints (aside from mild mechanical effusion) and hypermobile presentation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient suffered an acute atraumatic joint subluxation resulting in a severe, incapacitating pain flare and transient loss of mobility. Urgent after-hours medical assessment was strictly necessary to rule out a sustained dislocation, fracture, or neurovascular compromise that would mandate immediate emergency department transfer. The patient's inability to safely weight-bear required in-home physical examination and the immediate provision of stronger analgesia to restore baseline function and prevent falls during the night. The acuity of the functional decline and severity of the pain meant management could not be safely deferred to routine daytime hours, fulfilling the requirements for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed short course of oral NSAIDs (Celecoxib 200mg daily) and paracetamol/codeine (500mg/30mg) strictly PRN for 3 days to break the acute pain cycle. Applied tubular compression bandage to right knee for proprioceptive feedback.
Positive Behavioral Change: Initiate daily closed-chain isometric leg exercises before getting out of bed. Successfully completing these low-impact movements will stabilize the joint, reducing the incidence of painful subluxations and rewarding the patient with improved daily confidence and mobility.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral for targeted joint stabilization and proprioceptive training.
DME/Safety (Optional): Removal of loose rugs.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 5 days for ongoing pain management plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if a joint dislocates and cannot be reduced, or if there is sudden loss of sensation, coldness, or blue discoloration in any limb.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute structural damage (e.g., meniscal tear) cannot be definitively excluded without imaging, though currently unlikely.
Clinical Course: Joint hypermobility is a lifelong biomechanical variant. While subluxations and pain flares are highly distressing, they are manageable with targeted muscle strengthening and joint awareness training.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate care if your knee or any other joint pops out and stays out, or if your foot becomes numb, cold, or pale.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The compression bandage provides your brain with better sensory feedback about where your knee is in space (proprioception), reducing clumsiness, while the anti-inflammatories settle the acute tissue irritation.
Actionable Steps: Wear supportive, flat footwear indoors instead of walking barefoot. The immediate stability will prevent further micro-trauma, reinforcing the habit of joint protection.

